# Arc Foundry — Debug and automation surface

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Arc Foundry carries a debugging and automation surface, `window.__foundry`, so the yard
can be posed and read from code without moving a real pointer or waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the build
installs the finished surface on `window.__foundry` as soon as the game has initialized.
Every scenario driven from code reaches the game through it, so it is present and exactly
as specified here.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Arc Foundry carries a debugging and automation surface so the yard can be posed and read
from code without moving a real pointer or waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the build's
`initialize` returns the finished surface beside the state it built, as the pair
`[state, debug]`. The engine returns that same value from `engine.debug`, and it is
reached that way alone: nothing is installed on the page. Every scenario driven from code
reaches the game through it, so it is present and exactly as specified here.
`src/game.ts` declares and exports its type, `FoundryDebugApi`.
{{else}}
Arc Foundry carries a debugging and automation surface so the yard can be posed and read
from code without moving a real pointer or waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the game
instance's `initialize` returns the finished surface. The engine holds it and returns it
from `engine.debug`, and it is reached that way alone: nothing is installed on the page.
Every scenario driven from code reaches the game through it, so it is present and exactly
as specified here. `src/game.ts` declares and exports its type, `FoundryDebugApi`.
{{/if}}

Each operation is a reading or a pose. A pose arranges the yard through the same systems
play uses: a placed rock rolls through the real press, a harvested candidate becomes a
component through the real harvest, a combine resolves through the real combine, and a
released unit walks the real pathfinder. A pose establishes a precondition and never an
outcome; what happens next comes from advancing the real simulation.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls it.

## A deterministic core

The surface rests on the simulation being deterministic: every rate integrated against
the delta time the game is given, so an interval of simulation time reaches the same
state however it was divided into frames. Two further properties make it driveable from
code:

- Render-free core. The game advances from the elapsed time it is handed, independent of
  a canvas, of the frame loop that measured it, and of wall-clock time. Movement,
  pathfinding, firing, projectile travel, the status effects, and the economy all advance
  in that update. The dependency runs one way: the simulation reads nothing from the
  renderer.
- Seeded randomness. Every random draw runs off a generator seeded by `reset`, and the
  game keeps its whole generator state where `reset` can restore it. The press's type and
  quality rolls, each wave's composition and spawn order, and every crit roll all come
  off it.

Given the same seed and the same sequence of calls and elapsed simulation time, the game
reaches the same state every time.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The keyboard, the pointer, and the debug overlay belong to the runtime layer beneath the
game. The surface carries no operation for the registered actions or for the overlay: it
carries the raw input operations below instead, because a caller works in the stage's
logical units and the runtime layer is what maps a device position onto them.

The clock is the runtime layer's too, but nothing outside this build owns it, so the
surface is what takes the game off real time and advances it, through `setAutoStep` and
`advance` below.
{{else}}
The clock, the keyboard, the pointer, and the debug overlay belong to the {{engine.name}}
engine, which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the root of this repository,
and the surface carries no operation for any of them. The engine advances the game frame
by frame and can take it off real time, it drives the actions the game registers, it
reports the pointer position in the stage's logical units along with the press and
release edges, and it draws and toggles the debug overlay.

A caller reaching a control therefore dispatches a real pointer or key event at the
engine's surface and advances a frame, and the game handles it exactly as it handles a
player's.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Arc Foundry's, under Diagnostics below.

## The operations

The surface carries `version` (`FOUNDRY_DEBUG_VERSION`, `3`), a plain number, and the
operations below. Values are plain numbers, strings, and booleans. `x` and `y` are in the
stage's logical units, `col` and `row` are tile coordinates, and an `id` is a structure's
or a unit's identifier as `snapshot` reports it.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Each operation takes only the parameters its table names, and an operation whose name
carries no parameter list takes none. A pose returns nothing; a reading returns what it
read and changes nothing.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Each operation's first parameter is the current state, `DeepReadonly<FoundryState>`,
before the parameters its table names, so an operation whose name carries no parameter
list takes the state alone. A pose returns the next `FoundryState`, built from
the one it was handed, which it leaves as it was; a reading returns what it read. A
caller drives a pose through the engine's `apply`, as
`engine.apply((s) => debug.setCharge(s, 500))`, and a reading against the engine's
current state, as `debug.snapshot(engine.state)`.
{{else}}
Each operation takes only the parameters its table names, and an operation whose name
carries no parameter list takes none. Each acts on the live world the moment it is
called. A pose returns nothing; a reading returns what it read and changes
nothing.
{{/if}}

An operation naming an id that no live structure or unit carries does nothing. An
operation whose arguments are outside the ranges this specification states fails loudly
rather than guessing what was meant.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### The clock

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setAutoStep(enabled)` | Takes the game off real time and gives it back. `setAutoStep(false)` stops the frame loop advancing the simulation from the wall clock, so the game changes only when `advance` says so. `setAutoStep(true)` returns it to running itself, which is how a build starts and how it is played. Drawing is unaffected either way. |
| `advance(seconds, frames)` | Runs `frames` whole frames covering `seconds` of simulation time, each worth `seconds / frames`, immediately and in order. `frames` defaults to `1`. Each is a real frame, the same update the loop runs followed by a render, so the game's own systems produce the result and the canvas reflects it. |

Every rate is per second and integrated against the frame's delta time, so
`advance(1, 1)` and `advance(1, 60)` cover the same second of simulation time and reach
the same outcome, beyond the drift a change in step size explains. The speed multiplier
scales what `advance` covers exactly as it scales a real frame.

{{/if}}
### Readings

| Operation | Returns |
| --- | --- |
| `snapshot` | The plain object under Snapshot shape below. |
| `panelButtons` | The build panel inspector's action controls for the selected structure, in slot order. |
| `menuButtons` | The choices of the menu screen currently showing, in the order they are presented. |
| `statusControls` | The status bar's speed, pause, and mute controls, in the order they are drawn. |

`panelButtons` and `menuButtons` each return an array of
`{ action, label, x, y, w, h, disabled }`. `label` is the text as drawn, `x`, `y`, `w`,
and `h` are the control's rectangle on the `1280 x 720` stage, and `disabled` reports
whether it currently ignores activation. `panelButtons` returns an empty array when the
inspector is showing no structure's actions, and `menuButtons` returns an empty array on
any screen that is not a menu, which includes `playing` under an in-place pause.

`statusControls` returns an array of `{ action, label, x, y, w, h, state }`, with the same
geometry and a `state` reporting the value the control is currently reading rather than
whether it can be activated. It returns an empty array on any screen with no status bar.

| Reading | `action` values |
| --- | --- |
| `panelButtons` | `keep`, `downgrade`, `combine`, `comborecipe`, `comboupgrade`, `targeting`, `remove` |
| `menuButtons` | `salvage`, `howto`, `map-substation`, `map-switchyard`, `map-transformer`, `difficulty-easy`, `difficulty-medium`, `difficulty-hard`, `back`, `resume`, `restart`, `quit`, `again`, `menu` |
| `statusControls` | `speed`, `pause`, `mute` |

| `statusControls` action | `state` |
| --- | --- |
| `speed` | The live multiplier, one of `1`, `2`, `4`, `8` |
| `pause` | `true` while the in-place pause is engaged |
| `mute` | `true` while audio is muted |

The rectangle a reading reports is the control's real hit region: a press and release at
the center of a reported, non-disabled rectangle activates that control. Because
`specs/ui.md` fixes each menu's content and navigation rather than its layout, this is
how a caller finds a choice without knowing where it was drawn. A `statusControls`
`state` and what the control draws agree, so toggling mute changes both the reported
value and the pixels inside the reported rectangle.

All four readings are pure. Reading a menu never moves the highlight and never activates
a choice.

### The run

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `reset(options)` | Returns the game to its title state. `options` is optional, and `options.seed` seeds every random draw, defaulting to `DEFAULT_SEED` (`1`). It clears the map and difficulty back to their defaults, empties the yard, and returns Charge, Grid Integrity, refinement, the wave counter, the Maze Rating, and the simulation clock to their opening values. The mute bit is untouched. |
| `setMap(map)` | Sets the map the next run opens on: `"substation"`, `"switchyard"`, or `"transformer"`. It defaults to `"substation"`. |
| `setDifficulty(difficulty)` | Sets the difficulty the next run opens at: `"easy"`, `"medium"`, or `"hard"`. It defaults to `"medium"`. |
| `startRun` | Begins a run on the current map at the current difficulty, exactly as confirming the difficulty select does, and opens it on its first build phase. |
| `setScreen(screen)` | Moves to a named screen, exactly as reaching it in play does. `screen` is one of the eight identifiers in `specs/ui.md`. |
| `setMenuIndex(index)` | Sets the highlighted entry on the menu screen showing, counted from `0`. |
| `setPaused(paused)` | Engages or releases the in-place pause. |
| `setSpeed(multiplier)` | Sets the speed multiplier to `1`, `2`, `4`, or `8`. |
| `setOverlay(overlay, open)` | Opens or closes a read-only overlay. `overlay` is `"combos"` or `"damage"`. |

### Resources and progress

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setCharge(amount)` | Sets the current Charge. |
| `setIntegrity(amount)` | Sets the current Grid Integrity, without resolving a defeat by itself; the game's own rules resolve on the next advance. |
| `setRefinement(level)` | Sets the refinement level, `0` through `8`, and with it the roll odds. |
| `setWave(n)` | Sets the current wave number, so units released from now on scale to wave `n`. |
| `setStamps(n)` | Sets the stamps remaining in the level's allowance, `0` through `5`. |

### Structures

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `clearStructures` | Removes every candidate, component, combination tower, and blocker from the yard, reopens their tiles, clears the selection and the combine set, and recomputes the route. |
| `setNextRoll(type, quality)` | Arms the exact component the next placed rock rolls. `type` is a base component identifier and `quality` is `1` through `5`. The rock still enters through the real placement path; this fixes only what it rolls. |
| `clearNextRoll` | Clears the arming and returns the press to its seeded random roll. |
| `placeRock(col, row)` | Drops a rock at the anchor tile through the real placement path: it rolls, becomes a candidate, spends one stamp, and the route is recomputed. It is refused exactly as a pointer press would be when the footprint is illegal, and the refusal is readable in the snapshot. |
| `placeComponent(type, quality, col, row)` | Places a permanent firing component of that type at that quality, at the anchor tile, as though it had been harvested there. It spends no stamp, consumes no harvest, and starts no wave. |
| `placeCombo(combo, col, row)` | Places a combination tower at upgrade level `0` at the anchor tile. `combo` is a tower identifier. |
| `placeBlocker(col, row)` | Places an inert blocker at the anchor tile. |
| `select(id)` | Selects a structure, as a pointer press on it would. |
| `clearSelection` | Clears the selection and the combine set. |
| `addToCombineSet(id)` | Adds one base structure to the explicit combine set, or removes it when it is already in the set. |
| `clearCombineSet` | Empties the explicit combine set and leaves the selection as it is. |
| `keep(id)` | Harvests a candidate as a permanent component. It is the level's harvest, so it starts the wave. |
| `downgrade(id)` | Harvests a candidate as a permanent component one quality tier lower. It is the harvest, so it starts the wave. |
| `combine(id)` | Commits a combine from the initiating structure: a quality fold of a matching pair, or a reachable recipe. With a combine set it folds exactly that set; otherwise it resolves the ingredients itself, preferring a candidate over a standing structure. It starts the wave when it consumes a candidate and leaves the phase running when it folds standing structures only. |
| `dismantle(id)` | Removes a structure, reopens its tiles, and recomputes the route. |
| `setTargeting(id, priority)` | Sets a firing structure's targeting priority to one of the five. |
| `upgradeQuality` | Buys the next refinement level for Charge. |
| `upgradeCombo(id)` | Raises a combination tower's upgrade level by one, for Charge. |

The three `place` operations pose a yard directly, so a scenario stands up exactly the
structures its requirement is about. Each is refused on an illegal footprint exactly as
`placeRock` is.

### The Load

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `clearUnits` | Removes every live unit from the yard without paying a bounty or costing Grid Integrity. |
| `clearProjectiles` | Removes every projectile in flight without applying its damage. |
| `spawnUnit(type)` | Releases one unit at the map's entry through the real spawner, scaled to the current wave. `type` is `"mote"`, `"spark"`, `"slug"`, `"cluster"`, `"filament"`, `"dynamo"`, or `"overload"`. |
| `setUnitPosition(id, x, y)` | Moves a live unit to a logical position. |
| `setUnitWaypoint(id, index)` | Sets the chain node a live unit is heading for, `1` through `k + 1`. |
| `setUnitHp(id, hp)` | Sets a live unit's current health, clamped to at least `1` and at most its maximum. It never changes the maximum, so the unit stays the same type at the same wave scaling and its health bar reads the fraction it is on. The Overload Dynamo takes no health change. |
| `setUnitFrozen(id, frozen)` | Holds a live unit still, or releases it. A frozen unit does not move and does not advance along the chain, and everything else about it continues: it is targetable, it takes damage, its burn ticks, and its slow expires. |

A released unit is live from that moment. The next advance moves it, fires at it, and
resolves its leak at the collector, exactly as a unit of a composed wave. Releasing one
from a build phase therefore puts the run into a live wave, so the phase reads `"wave"`,
or `"finale"` for the Overload Dynamo, and the floor runs. Nothing else is scheduled to
spawn, so the released units are alone on the yard. The build phase's own harvest is not
consumed and no wave number is spent. This is the one control operation that starts the
floor running.

A wave otherwise begins the way normal play begins one, by committing the level's
harvest.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### Input

These feed the same input path the runtime layer feeds, so a posed press and a player's
press are the same event to the game. Nothing is bypassed: the hit regions, the placement
rules, and the phase restrictions all run exactly as they do for a player. Each takes
effect immediately, when it is called, rather than being sampled once per update.

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `pointerMove(x, y)` | Moves the pointer to a logical position, updating the held footprint and the hover state. |
| `click(x, y)` | A press and release at a logical position. |
| `rightClick(x, y)` | A right press and release at a logical position. |
| `shiftClick(x, y)` | A press and release with shift held. |
| `keyDown(code)` | Presses a key down. `code` is a standard `KeyboardEvent.code`. Any one-shot action it fires on the current screen applies immediately. |
| `keyUp(code)` | Releases a key. |
| `press(code)` | A `keyDown` immediately followed by a `keyUp`. |

{{/if}}
## Snapshot shape

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
`snapshot` returns:
{{else}}
`snapshot()` returns:
{{/if}}

```js
{
  version: 3,
  screen: "title" | "mapselect" | "difficultyselect" | "howto"
        | "playing" | "paused" | "victory" | "overload",
  phase: "build" | "wave" | "finale" | null,   // null off the yard
  menuIndex: <number>,           // the highlighted menu entry, from 0
  paused: <boolean>,             // the in-place pause; screen stays "playing"
  map: "substation" | "switchyard" | "transformer",
  difficulty: "easy" | "medium" | "hard",
  wave: <number>,                // current wave, 0 before wave 1
  totalWaves: <number>,          // N for the chosen difficulty
  waveActive: <boolean>,         // a wave is spawning or has live units
  charge: <number>,
  integrity: <number>,
  refinement: <number>,          // 0..8
  qualityOdds: [<t1>, <t2>, <t3>, <t4>, <t5>],  // the live roll odds, summing to 1
  nextRoll: { type: <string>, quality: <number> } | null,
  stampsLeft: <number>,          // of the 5-per-level allowance
  speed: 1 | 2 | 4 | 8,
  muted: <boolean>,
  overlays: { combos: <boolean>, damage: <boolean> },
  mazeLength: <number>,          // the ground route through the chain, in tiles
  mazeRating: <number>,          // damage tallied on the Overload Dynamo
  selected: <id> | null,
  combineSet: [<id>, ...],
  held: { active: <boolean>, col: <number>, row: <number>, legal: <boolean> },
  entry: { col: <number>, row: <number> },
  collector: { col: <number>, row: <number> },
  waypoints: [{ index: <number>, col: <number>, row: <number> }],  // ordered, 1..k
  units: [
    {
      id: <id>,
      type: "mote"|"spark"|"slug"|"cluster"|"filament"|"dynamo"|"overload",
      x: <number>, y: <number>,
      hp: <number>, maxHp: <number>,
      speed: <number>,           // current speed, after any slow
      baseSpeed: <number>,       // the roster speed
      flying: <boolean>,
      frozen: <boolean>,
      waypointIndex: <number>,   // the chain node it is heading for, 1..k+1
      progress: <number>,        // remaining route length to that node, in tiles
      slowFactor: <number>, slowUntil: <number>,
      burnDps: <number>, burnUntil: <number>,
      invincible: <boolean>,     // the Overload Dynamo
    },
  ],
  structures: [
    {
      id: <id>,
      kind: "candidate" | "component" | "combo" | "blocker",
      type: <string> | null,     // component type or combination tower id; null for a blocker
      quality: <number> | null,  // 1..5 for a base structure; null otherwise
      level: <number> | null,    // 0..3 for a combination tower; null otherwise
      col: <number>, row: <number>,
      cx: <number>, cy: <number>,
      range: <number>, damage: <number>, fireRate: <number>,
      targeting: "first"|"last"|"nearest"|"strongest"|"weakest" | null,
      heading: <number>,         // the firing head's rotation, in radians
      firing: <boolean>,
      kills: <number>, damageDealt: <number>,
      auraRadius: <number>, auraBonus: <number>,
      abilities: [<string>, ...],
    },
  ],
  projectiles: [
    { id: <id>, x: <number>, y: <number>, vx: <number>, vy: <number>,
      type: <string>, heading: <number>, damage: <number>, targetId: <id> | null },
  ],
  simTime: <number>,             // the simulation clock, in seconds
}
```

The shape is fixed and every field is present on every screen. A field the current screen
does not use reports its resting value rather than going missing.

| Field | Resting value |
| --- | --- |
| `phase` | `null` off the yard |
| `menuIndex` | `0` on `playing` |
| `wave` | `0` before wave `1` |
| `mazeRating` | `0` before the finale |
| `selected`, `nextRoll` | `null` |
| `combineSet`, `units`, `structures`, `projectiles` | empty |
| `held` | `{ active: false, col: 0, row: 0, legal: false }` |

- A `structures` entry covers all four things that occupy the grid. A `blocker` reports
  `type: null` and zero `damage` and `range`. A Regulator reports its `auraRadius` and
  `auraBonus` with zero `damage`. A structure that carries no aura reports `0` for both.
- `damage` is the structure's effective per-shot damage, including any aura buff on it,
  unrounded.
- `kills` and `damageDealt` are the running tallies of `specs/components.md`.
- `progress` is the remaining length of the unit's route to the node it is heading for,
  in tiles, so `waypointIndex` and `progress` together are the ordering `first` and
  `last` select on.
- The chain is numbered from `1`. `waypoints` runs `1` through `k`, the order numbers
  drawn on the yard, and a unit's `waypointIndex` is the node it is heading for in that
  same numbering: `1` while it walks from the entry to `WP1`, and `k + 1` once it is past
  the last waypoint and heading for the collector. It never decreases.
- `qualityOdds` is the five-tier distribution at the current refinement level, the same
  odds the build panel shows.
- `muted` mirrors the mute bit the runtime holds, refreshed on every update.
- `simTime` is the simulation clock of `specs/controls.md`, so it does not advance while
  paused or off the `playing` screen.

Every field is read straight off the game's own state or derived from it at the call, so
what the snapshot reports is what the game holds.

## Diagnostics

The debug overlay shows the values the game registers with it as diagnostic sources.
Register at least the current `screen`, `phase`, and `paused` flag, the wave as `n / N`,
the `charge`, `integrity`, and `refinement`, the stamps remaining, the speed multiplier,
the maze length, the live unit count, the structure count, and the selected structure's
id and its damage and range. Keep each short enough to read on a line, and keep every
source a pure read, so watching the overlay leaves the game as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered sources, it
is shown and hidden by the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`), it is off
until toggled, and it reads the game without changing it. Keep it visually plain and
clearly separate from the game's own display.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Registering those values is the whole of Arc Foundry's part. Each source is called with
the state current at the read, as `DeepReadonly<FoundryState>`, and reads off that
argument. Drawing the panel, toggling it, and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Arc Foundry's part, through the world's
diagnostics in the game mode's `beginPlay`. Each source is a function of no arguments,
called at each read; read the live state at the call, through the world the mode holds,
so the panel reports the frame being drawn. Drawing the panel and toggling it are the
engine's.
{{/if}}
